{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 45,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"}
      ],
      "why": "Fully pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax; it is the reference family for the 50 MSPS floor and anchors the high-accuracy end of the front. Sweep the accuracy knobs (W, N, guards, rounding) broadly since accuracy is the second objective."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 55,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "Registering every m stages cuts flip-flops (the dominant power term) while still emitting 1 result/cycle; because the throughput floor is only 50 MHz there is room to raise m and lower power until Fmax drops below 50 MHz. This is the most likely min-power corner, so it gets the larger share."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The 50 MSPS constraint forces ~1 result/cycle at >=50 MHz, which only the pipelined families can plausibly deliver (iterative/unrolled would need impractically high Fmax at useful N). Accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) is set by data_width/n_iter/guards/rounding, identical across families, so both plans sweep those knobs over a wide box to expose the accuracy-vs-power trade-off. pipelined_m is weighted slightly higher because the low 50 MHz floor lets large m trade Fmax for fewer FFs, which is where the minimum power_index likely lives; pipelined covers the high-accuracy/high-Fmax corner and guards against pipelined_m failing the throughput constraint."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round: the feasible front already satisfies every spec constraint and covers the needed region. Constraint evidence — throughput_msps >= 50 is met by all 7 Pareto designs (64.3-123 MSPS, best seen 171), and max_abs_err <= 0.000244141 is met with margin by all of them (worst front point 2.37e-4 = 2^-12.04, best 1.17e-5 = 2^-16.38), so nothing here is infeasible. Objective evidence — the front spans power_index 2.45..5.67 against HV ref 40 and accuracy_bits 12..16.4 against HV ref 12, i.e. it already brackets both HV reference points, giving HV 160.1. The selection rule (min power_index) lands on pipelined_m [data_width=16 n_iter=16 angle_guard=1 frac_guard=3 rounding=trunc m=2]: power_index=2.45, accuracy_bits=12.0, max_abs_err=2.37e-4 (constraint-compliant), throughput 75.4 MSPS (>50), area 34902 um^2, 513 FFs. pipelined supplies the high-accuracy tail (up to 16.38 bits) while pipelined_m supplies the low-power head, so the trade-off curve is covered; no unexplored family remains in the registry to add. Only 60/100 evals were used, but the round cap binds and any further refinement decision would be treated as stop, so I stop with the front as-is."}
